        "\n",
        "### [Comparison with R]{style=\"color:blue;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| Feature             | Python                | R                             |\n",
        "|--------------------|----------------------|------------------------------|\n",
        "| Install package     | `pip install seaborn` | `install.packages(\"ggplot2\")` |\n",
        "| Load package        | `import seaborn`      | `library(ggplot2)`            |\n",
        "| When install needed | Once                  | Once                          |\n",
        "| When load needed    | Every session         | Every session                 |\n",
        "| Keyword difference  | `import`              | `library()`                   |\n",
        "\n",
        "::: callout-note\n",
        "## Importing Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "Importing functions from packages is the same as importing functions from modules. The syntax remains identical; only the source (module or package) changes.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "### Creating and Accessing Packages\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  Create a Directory: create a folder that will act as the package root.\n",
        "2.  Add Modules: Inside the directory, add Python files (modules). Each module can contain related functions or classes.\n",
        "3.  Add **init**.py: add an **init**.py file to the directory. This file tells Python that the directory should be treated as a package.\n",
        "4.  Create Sub-packages (Optional): One can organize code further by creating subdirectories with their own **init**.py files.\n",
        "5.  Import Modules: Modules or functions inside the package can be imported using dot notation, for example:`from mypackage.module1 import greet`"
